WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.420
Learn how to add a policy route to a VPC. Policy routes act on the VPC router itself, so a single rule can block one subnet from another, for every virtual machine on them.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:20.270
The VPC demo-3tier routes between its web, app, and database subnets. Policy routes have no form field yet, so add one in the VPC's YAML.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.670
Open the action menu of demo-3tier,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.430
and select Edit YAML.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.850
The VPC's spec is still empty. It has no static routes and no policies.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:45.330
Add a policy route. Its action is drop, and its match selects traffic from the web subnet, 10.230.1.0/24, to the database subnet, 10.230.3.0/24.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:52.910
The priority must be higher than 31000, the priority of the routes that Kube-OVN adds for each subnet, or those routes win.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.690
Select Save.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.810
Open the console of demo-web.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.690
Log in as sles, with the password that cloud-init set.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:14.930
Request the app tier, then the database.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:23.810
The app tier answers, and the database request times out. The VPC router drops it before it reaches the database subnet.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:31.229
One policy route on the VPC router keeps the web tier away from the database, while the rest of the VPC routes as before.
